<div class="ncard"><a id="node-orsl-mutual-info-spark-discovery-01" name="node-orsl-mutual-info-spark-discovery-01"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1509/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">orsl-mutual-info-spark-discovery-01</td><td class="vd">Phase 1: Digital Spark &amp; First-Message Entro</td><td class="vd">Frontier</td><td class="cmp vd">C:81</td><td class="yld vd">Y:88</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Digital Spark Discovery: High-Entropy Signal Tokens &amp; Bayesian Compatibility Filtering</div><div class="ndesc">Quantifies why generic low-entropy greetings (&quot;hey&quot;) yield near-zero response rates while high-surprisal, context-anchored opening tokens referencing specific profile eigenvectors maximize Bayesian posterior interest.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">P(\text{Reply} \mid m_1, \mathcal{P}_B) = \sigma\left( \beta_0 + \beta_1 I(m_1; \mathcal{P}_B) - \beta_2 \|\text{Length}(m_1) - L^*\|^2 \rig</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> High-Mutual-Information Opener &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
