<table class="sec-ban" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0">
<tr>
<td width="64%" class="vd">
<div class="sec-kicker">CATEGORY 60 &bull; Periorbital Canthal Geometry &amp; CIE L*a*b* Egyptian-Irish Ocular Contrast Tensor (Diagram OS-14)</div>
<div class="sec-title">OSINT Beauty Science, Periorbital Anthropometrics &amp; Optimized Egyptian-Irish Ocular Aesthetics</div>
<div class="sec-desc">Quantitative facial photogrammetry and psychophysics of the high-contrast Egyptian-Irish ocular phenotype: CIE L*a*b* luminance/chromatic contrast between light green/blue/hazel irises and dark eumelanin kohl/limbal framing, positive canthal tilt geometry, and Tyndall stromal scattering.</div>
</td>
<td width="12%" class="tc vd"><div class="sec-kicker">NODES</div><div class="sec-val">14</div></td>
<td width="12%" class="tc vd"><div class="sec-kicker">MEAN C</div><div class="sec-val">89.4</div></td>
<td width="12%" class="tc"><div class="sec-kicker">MEAN Y</div><div class="sec-val">92.1</div></td>
</tr>
</table>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-celtic-mediterranean-contrast-01" name="node-oaei-celtic-mediterranean-contrast-01"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1565/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-celtic-mediterranean-contrast-01</td><td class="vd">Ocular Phenotype Psychophysics</td><td class="vd">Frontier</td><td class="cmp vd">C:81</td><td class="yld vd">Y:88</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">The &quot;Egyptian-Irish&quot; High-Contrast Ocular Phenotype: Pale Stroma vs. Eumelanin Framing</div><div class="ndesc">Quantifies the rare visual strikingness of the &quot;Egyptian-Irish&quot; ocular archetype: combining a low-melanin Rayleigh-scattering pale green, aquamarine, or ice-blue iris (Celtic OCA2/HERC2 allele expression) with dense, jet-black eumelanin cilia (lashes), defined limbal ring, and elongated almond canthal framing (Mediterranean/Nilotic kohl architecture).</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">C_{\text{Michelson}} = \frac{L_{\text{iris}} - L_{\text{limbal/lash}}}{L_{\text{iris}} + L_{\text{limbal/lash}}} \to 0.88, \quad S_{gaze} = </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> OCA2-Eumelanin Contrast Disjunction &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-cie-lab-chromatic-induction-02" name="node-oaei-cie-lab-chromatic-induction-02"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1566/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-cie-lab-chromatic-induction-02</td><td class="vd">Colorimetry &amp; Visual Psychophysics</td><td class="vd">Advanced</td><td class="cmp vd">C:85</td><td class="yld vd">Y:91</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">CIE L*a*b* Opponent-Color Chromatic Induction: Terracotta/Bronze vs. Emerald-Cyan Irises</div><div class="ndesc">Applies Ewald Hering’s opponent-process color theory in CIE L*a*b* space: framing green/aquamarine (-a*) or blue (-b*) irises with warm Egyptian copper, terracotta, burnished gold, or warm burgundy (+a*, +b*) induces simultaneous chromatic contrast that boosts perceived iris saturation by 35%.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">\mathbf{c}_{\text{perceived}}^{\text{iris}} = \mathbf{c}_{\text{true}}^{\text{iris}} - \kappa_{\text{ind}} \left( \mathbf{c}_{\text{surround</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Hering Opponent Induction &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-canthal-tilt-palpebral-geometry-03" name="node-oaei-canthal-tilt-palpebral-geometry-03"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1567/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-canthal-tilt-palpebral-geometry-03</td><td class="vd">Craniofacial Ocular Anthropometrics</td><td class="vd">Expert</td><td class="cmp vd">C:89</td><td class="yld vd">Y:94</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Positive Canthal Tilt (+5° to +8°) &amp; Almond Palpebral Fissure Aspect-Ratio Photogrammetry</div><div class="ndesc">Measures the intercanthal-to-lateral-canthal vector angle theta_CT and palpebral fissure width-to-height ratio (W_pf / H_pf ~ 3.0–3.4): subtle upward lateral vector framing elongates the medial-to-lateral axis into the classic feline/Nefertiti almond silhouette.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">\theta_{CT} = \arctan\left( \frac{y_{\text{lat}} - y_{\text{med}}}{x_{\text{lat}} - x_{\text{med}}} \right) \in [+4^\circ, +8^\circ], \quad </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Lateral Canthal Vector Extension &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-egyptian-kohl-medial-optics-04" name="node-oaei-egyptian-kohl-medial-optics-04"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1568/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-egyptian-kohl-medial-optics-04</td><td class="vd">Historical &amp; Optical Biophysics</td><td class="vd">Frontier</td><td class="cmp vd">C:93</td><td class="yld vd">Y:97</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Ancient Egyptian Kohl (Mesdemet) Medial-Canthus Elongation &amp; Glare-Diffraction Physics</div><div class="ndesc">Analyzes the optical physics of classical Egyptian kohl framing: darkening the inner tear-duct caruncle vector and upper/lower mucocutaneous margins absorbs stray peripheral scleral glare, increases Modulation Transfer Function (MTF) contrast, and visually widens inter-ocular balance.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">\text{MTF}_{ocular}(\nu) = \frac{M_{image}(\nu)}{M_{object}(\nu)} \cdot \left( 1 - \frac{I_{\text{veiling\_glare}} (1 - \alpha_{\text{kohl}}</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Periorbital Baffle Optics &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-limbal-ring-corneal-clarity-05" name="node-oaei-limbal-ring-corneal-clarity-05"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1569/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-limbal-ring-corneal-clarity-05</td><td class="vd">Biometric Youth &amp; Vitality Signaling</td><td class="vd">Expert</td><td class="cmp vd">C:89</td><td class="yld vd">Y:88</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Limbal Ring Annular Contrast &amp; Scleral Whiteness CIE Whiteness Index (WI)</div><div class="ndesc">Quantifies Peshek’s Limbal Ring perceptual cue: a sharp, dark corneal-scleral limbal annulus contrasted against a high-whiteness, low-redness (low a*) sclera serves as a universal cross-cultural biomarker of vascular health and youthfulness.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">\text{WI}_{CIE} = Y + 800(x_n - x) + 1700(y_n - y), \quad \Delta L_{\text{limbal}} = \frac{1}{2\pi} \oint_{\partial R_{\text{iris}}} \left| </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Limbal Annular Gradient &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-tyndall-rayleigh-iris-scattering-06" name="node-oaei-tyndall-rayleigh-iris-scattering-06"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1570/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-tyndall-rayleigh-iris-scattering-06</td><td class="vd">Biophysical Tissue Optics</td><td class="vd">Advanced</td><td class="cmp vd">C:93</td><td class="yld vd">Y:91</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Rayleigh &amp; Tyndall Stromal Light Scattering in Green/Blue/Hazel Irises under Off-Axis Lighting</div><div class="ndesc">Models why blue and green Irish irises contain zero blue or green pigment—their color arises from Rayleigh/Tyndall structural scattering of short-wavelength light in the translucent anterior stroma over a dark retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), glowing intensely under 35-degree side-key illumination.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">I_{scat}(\lambda, \theta) = I_0(\lambda) \cdot \frac{1 + \cos^2\theta}{2 r^2} \cdot N_{stroma} \left( \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \right)^4 \left| </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Tangential Stromal Transillumination &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-mediapipe-468-golden-ratio-07" name="node-oaei-mediapipe-468-golden-ratio-07"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1571/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-mediapipe-468-golden-ratio-07</td><td class="vd">Computer-Vision OSINT Anthropometrics</td><td class="vd">Frontier</td><td class="cmp vd">C:97</td><td class="yld vd">Y:94</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">468-Vertex Mediapipe 3D Facial Mesh Photogrammetry &amp; Periorbital Phi (1.618) Harmonics</div><div class="ndesc">Extracts 3D periorbital landmark tensors from standard portrait imagery using a 468-vertex facial mesh to compute interpupillary distance (IPD), ESR (Eye Separation Ratio ~ 0.45–0.47 of bizygomatic width), and brow-arch Golden Ratio apex placement.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">\text{ESR} = \frac{\|\mathbf{p}_{pupil, L} - \mathbf{p}_{pupil, R}\|_2}{W_{\text{bizygomatic}}} \in [0.45, 0.475], \quad \frac{x_{\text{apex</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Phi Brow-Apex Ratio (0.618) &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-celtic-nile-undertone-matrix-08" name="node-oaei-celtic-nile-undertone-matrix-08"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1572/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-celtic-nile-undertone-matrix-08</td><td class="vd">Spectrophotometric Color Harmony</td><td class="vd">Advanced</td><td class="cmp vd">C:83</td><td class="yld vd">Y:97</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Spectral Skin-Reflectance Undertone Matrix: Cool Celtic Alabaster vs. Warm Golden-Olive Harmony</div><div class="ndesc">Decomposes cutaneous spectral reflectance R(lambda) into hemoglobin (542/577 nm peaks) and melanin absorption curves to harmonize periorbital metallic/matte pigments across cool pink-porcelain Irish undertones and warm golden Egyptian accents.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">\frac{K(\lambda)}{S(\lambda)} = \frac{(1 - R_\infty(\lambda))^2}{2 R_\infty(\lambda)}, \quad K(\lambda) = c_{mel} \varepsilon_{mel}(\lambda)</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Kubelka-Munk Chromophore Balance &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-purkinje-catchlight-geometry-09" name="node-oaei-purkinje-catchlight-geometry-09"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1573/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-purkinje-catchlight-geometry-09</td><td class="vd">Ophthalmic Specular Optics</td><td class="vd">Expert</td><td class="cmp vd">C:87</td><td class="yld vd">Y:88</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">First Purkinje Corneal Specular Catchlight Geometry &amp; Ocular Liveliness Optics</div><div class="ndesc">Calculates the exact spherical convex-mirror reflection (First Purkinje Image) on the anterior tear film (radius r_c ~ 7.8 mm): positioning a soft ring/octabox key light at 10-o’clock or 2-o’clock elevation places a crisp catchlight on the upper iris border without occluding the pupil.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">d_{\text{Purkinje}} = -\frac{r_{\text{cornea}}}{2} \approx -3.9\text{ mm}, \quad R_{\text{Fresnel}}(\theta_i \approx 0) = \left( \frac{n_{\t</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> First Purkinje Catchlight &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-pupillary-aperture-contrast-10" name="node-oaei-pupillary-aperture-contrast-10"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1574/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-pupillary-aperture-contrast-10</td><td class="vd">Autonomic Ocular Psychophysics</td><td class="vd">Frontier</td><td class="cmp vd">C:91</td><td class="yld vd">Y:91</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Hess Pupillary Aperture Aesthetics &amp; Ambient Illuminance Balance (150–300 Lux)</div><div class="ndesc">Balances ambient lighting illuminance E_lux so pupillary diameter d_pupil sits in the optimal 4.0–5.2 mm aesthetic window—large enough to signal Eckhard Hess warmth and attraction while preserving a wide, visible annulus of colored Irish iris stroma.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">d_{pupil}(L, a) = 7.75 - 5.75 \left[ \frac{(L \cdot a / 846)^{0.41}}{(L \cdot a / 846)^{0.41} + 2} \right], \quad \frac{A_{\text{iris\_visib</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Hess-Watson Aperture Sweet Spot &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-supraorbital-tarsal-compactness-11" name="node-oaei-supraorbital-tarsal-compactness-11"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1575/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-supraorbital-tarsal-compactness-11</td><td class="vd">Periorbital Structural Depth</td><td class="vd">Expert</td><td class="cmp vd">C:87</td><td class="yld vd">Y:94</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Supraorbital Brow-to-Lash Vertical Compactness &amp; Tarsal Fold Shadow-Gradient Control</div><div class="ndesc">Optimizes perceived upper-orbital framing by placing matte low-luminance contour in the supraorbital crease and high-albedo satin sheen exclusively on the mobile tarsal plate, sculpting 3D depth through Lambertian shading.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">I(x,y) = \rho_{albedo}(x,y) \cdot I_0 \max\left(0,\, \mathbf{n}(x,y) \cdot \mathbf{s}_{light}\right) \implies \Delta z_{\text{perceived}}(x,</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Lambertian Tarsal Sculpting &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
<div class="ncard"><a id="node-oaei-bilateral-symmetry-anisotropic-12" name="node-oaei-bilateral-symmetry-anisotropic-12"></a><table class="nbar" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="vd"><b>#1576/1592</b></td><td class="tm vd">oaei-bilateral-symmetry-anisotropic-12</td><td class="vd">Precision Geometric Harmonization</td><td class="vd">Advanced</td><td class="cmp vd">C:91</td><td class="yld vd">Y:97</td><td class="vd">Eq:4|J:4</td><td class="tr"><a href="#toc-top" class="lnk">TOC</a></td></tr></table><div class="nbody"><div class="ntitle">Bilateral Periorbital Asymmetry Compensation via Anisotropic Line-Weight Balancing</div><div class="ndesc">Measures natural sub-millimeter left-right palpebral height asymmetry delta_H (present in 95% of human faces) and compensates by modulating upper-margin kohl line thickness by exact inverse delta_H so the net visible scleral/iris aperture is 100% bilaterally symmetric.</div><div class="nnotes"><b>Eq (4):</b> <span class="eqf">H_{\text{visible}, L} = H_{\text{anat}, L} - w_{\text{line}, L} \equiv H_{\text{anat}, R} - w_{\text{line}, R} = H_{\text{visible}, R} \impl</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Lexicon:</b> Aperture-Equalizing Line Weight &bull; OmniSingularity Invariant</div></div></div>
